1. Synchronous Communications
1. Rest Template
- Simplest implementation
- Service A -> HTTP Request -> Service B -> HTTP Response -> Service A Continues
- But now RestTemplate is Deprecated (due to its blocking nature) though it is still used.
- Instead WebClient is used for api calls to other service as it is non blocking.

Example: RestTemplate
	RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
	String response = restTemplate.getObject("http://order-service/order/123",String.class);
	System.out.println(response);

	Service B(Provider)
	@GetMapping("/order/{id}")
	public Order getOrder(@PathVariable String id) {
		return orderService.findOrder(id);
	}
-------------------

2. Web Client
- Modern, non blocking HTTP client introduced as part of Spring WebFlux.
- It can handle many concurrent requests with fewer threads, which is useful in high-scale systems.

Example:
WebClient webClient = webClient.create("http://localhost:8081");
Mono<String> response = webClient.get().uri("http://order-service/orders/12").retrieve().bodyToMono(Order.class)
-------------------

3. Open Feign/ Feign Client
Frameworks handle discovery + load balancing for you
e.g. Spring Boot + OpenFeign

@FeignClient("user-service")
public interface UserClient {
	@GetMapping("/user/{id}")
	User getUser(@PathVariable int id);
}

When to FeignClient
- Crud operations, Simple Service to service calls
- Simplicity, easy integration with Eureka, Load Balancing, Resilience4j	


Feign with or without Discovery Server like Eureka

a) Without Eureka
Feign You can use without discovery servers (Eureka) as below
Example:
@FeignClient(
    name = "order-service",
    url = "http://localhost:8081"
)
public interface OrderClient {
    @GetMapping("/orders/{id}")
    Order getOrder(@PathVariable Long id);
}

b) With Eureka
Feign with eureka:
Example
@FeignClient(name = "order-service")
public interface OrderClient {
    @GetMapping("/orders/{id}")
    Order getOrder(@PathVariable Long id);
}

Advantages of using feign with eureka:
- Dynamic service lookup
- Load balancing
- No hard‑coded URLs
- Better for microservices
